theseusd install --separate: the plan. Nothing is changed: --apply performs it, as root.
  operator:  ada (uid 1000), in theseus-ops
  binary:    $TMP/build/theseusd -> /usr/local/lib/theseus/theseusd
  config:    $TMP/ada/theseus.toml, copied to /etc/theseus/theseus.toml for the daemon to read; the unit's flags give the layout's paths
  migrate:   $TMP/old (its store, spool, config.last-good.toml, bindings.toml), only read

  create  group  theseus-ops
                 the operator's group: its members reach the daemon's sockets
  create  user   theseus (system; its own group; home /var/lib/theseus; shell /usr/sbin/nologin)
                 the runtime and its storage run as their own OS identity, so a job, run as you, cannot read or write them
  add     member ada of theseus-ops
                 so you reach the daemon's sockets
  create  dir    /var/lib/theseus theseus:theseus 0700
                 the store (WAL, index, blobs), the config's copy, and the bindings file
  create  dir    /etc/theseus root:theseus 0750
                 the token's directory: the daemon reads it, your jobs cannot
  create  file   /etc/theseus/op-token theseus:theseus 0600 (empty)
                 the 1Password service-account token's place; the installer never writes a token
  install file   /etc/theseus/theseus.toml root:theseus 0640 (from $TMP/ada/theseus.toml)
                 your config, where the daemon can read it
  create  dir    /usr/local/lib/theseus root:root 0755
                 the binary's directory
  install file   /usr/local/lib/theseus/theseusd root:root 0755 (from $TMP/build/theseusd)
                 the binary is part of the floor: the operator cannot replace it
  write   file   /etc/systemd/system/theseusd.service root:root 0644
                 the daemon as theseus
                 | # Written by `theseusd install --separate`. A later --apply rewrites it:
                 | # put your own changes in a drop-in (`sudo systemctl edit theseusd`).
                 | [Unit]
                 | Description=Theseus daemon, as its own user
                 | Wants=network-online.target
                 | After=network-online.target
                 | StartLimitIntervalSec=300
                 | StartLimitBurst=10
                 |
                 | [Service]
                 | Type=exec
                 | User=theseus
                 | Group=theseus
                 | # The group whose members reach the daemon's sockets.
                 | SupplementaryGroups=theseus-ops
                 | ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/theseus/theseusd --config /etc/theseus/theseus.toml --state-dir /var/lib/theseus --socket /run/theseus/theseus.sock --op-token-file /etc/theseus/op-token
                 | UMask=0077
                 | # SIGINT is the daemon's clean stop: it removes its socket and closes the
                 | # store. A stop signals the daemon alone, so running jobs finish, and the
                 | # next daemon reads their results from the spool, as a shell-started
                 | # daemon's stop leaves them.
                 | KillSignal=SIGINT
                 | KillMode=process
                 | Restart=on-failure
                 | RestartSec=1
                 | # The unit's cgroup is the daemon's own: each L0 job gets a cgroup of its
                 | # own inside it, with a process cap and an exact stop.
                 | Delegate=yes
                 | # Open files (theseus-7vtp): systemd's soft limit of 1,024 ended the daemon at
                 | # about a thousand connections. The daemon raises its own soft limit to this
                 | # at start and holds its connections to a ceiling under it.
                 | LimitNOFILE=65536
                 | # The tasks the unit may hold: the daemon's threads and children (under 2,000),
                 | # and the admission ceiling's 8 executions at a job's cap of 4,096 each, with
                 | # room above, so a fork bomb in one job meets its own cap first.
                 | TasksMax=49152
                 |
                 | [Install]
                 | WantedBy=multi-user.target
  write   file   /etc/systemd/user/theseus-job-host.service root:root 0644
                 the job host's user unit, disabled until step 22b; here rather than in your home, so root never writes where your jobs can
                 | # Written by `theseusd install --separate`. Leave it disabled until step 22b
                 | # gives theseusd its job-host role: until then its start fails.
                 | [Unit]
                 | Description=Theseus job host: the separated daemon's jobs, run as you
                 |
                 | [Service]
                 | Type=exec
                 | ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/theseus/theseusd job-host
                 | # A stop signals the host alone: its jobs finish, and their results
                 | # wait in the spool for the next host to relay.
                 | KillMode=process
                 | Restart=on-failure
                 | RestartSec=5
                 |
                 | [Install]
                 | WantedBy=default.target
  write   file   /etc/tmpfiles.d/theseus.conf root:root 0644
                 makes /run/theseus at each boot: /run is a tmpfs
                 | # Written by `theseusd install --separate`.
                 | # The daemon's sockets: theseus.sock, and the job host's jobs.sock.
                 | d /run/theseus 0750 theseus theseus-ops -
  create  dir    /run/theseus theseus:theseus-ops 0750
                 the sockets' directory: theseus.sock, and the job host's jobs.sock (22b)
  copy    state  $TMP/old -> /var/lib/theseus (theseus:theseus 0700/0600)
                 your stopped daemon's state; the source is only read, and never deleted

14 to do, 0 already done.

Then, by hand:
  - copy the token in: sudo install -o theseus -g theseus -m 0600 $TMP/ada/op-token /etc/theseus/op-token
  - the daemon runs `op` from systemd's PATH (/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin), and none is there: install the 1Password CLI system-wide, or add its directory in a drop-in (`sudo systemctl edit theseusd`: [Service] Environment=PATH=<its dir>:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin)
  - sudo systemctl daemon-reload
  - stop the daemon you run now (`theseus shutdown`): both would serve the web UI's port and Discord; then sudo systemctl enable --now theseusd.service
  - log in again (or `newgrp theseus-ops`) for your membership in theseus-ops to take effect
  - leave theseus-job-host.service disabled until step 22b
